.\" The .TH line is a placeholder: packaging/stamp.sh rewrites its date and
.\" version at build time, so neither is kept current here by hand.
.TH HI 1 "2026-09-07" "say-hi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hi \- your shell config, on every host you say hi to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hi
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RB [ \-\-plain | \-\-no\-plain ]
.RB [ \-\-mux | \-\-no\-mux ]
.RB [ \-\-keep | \-\-no\-keep | \-\-end ]
.I target
.RI [ command ...]
.br
.B hi
.br
.B hi \-\-doctor
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-problems ]
.RB [ \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR]
.RI [ ssh\-options ]
.RI [ target ]
.br
.B hi \-\-install
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-\-link " " {none|user|system}]
.RB [ \-\-shell " " \fIlist\fR]
.RB [ \-\-print\-rc ]
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-configure
.RB [ \-\-preset " " \fIname\fR]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-uninstall
.RB [ \-\-purge ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-update
.RI [ tag ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-add\-package
.I group pkg\fR[,...]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-remove\-package
.IR pkg ...
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-add\-tag
.I host tag
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-set\-color
.I type name color
.RI [ rrggbb ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-unset\-color
.I type name
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-plugins
.br
.B hi \-\-plugin\-off
.IR name ...
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-plugin\-on
.IR name ...
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-add\-plugin
.IR "group name file" ...
.RI [ key = value ...]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-remove\-plugin
.I name
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B hi \-\-preview
.RB { colors | packages | header }
.br
.BR "hi \-h" " | " \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hi
copies your
.B say-hi
configuration (prompt, colors, aliases, editor configs, connect header) to a
target, starts a session there, and removes every trace when the session ends,
abrupt disconnect included. A say-hi installed on the target is neither read
nor touched.
.PP
With a trailing
.IR command ,
hi runs it inside its session and exits: the command gets hi's aliases, PATH,
and environment, and a pty when your stdin is one. Only the command's output
goes to stdout; hi's progress and errors go to stderr. For a plain, pty\-free
remote command (a tarball piped into a file, say), use
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
An ssh target with neither
.BR base64 (1)
nor
.BR openssl (1),
or nowhere to make a
.BR mktemp (1)
directory, gets its own login session instead, with a notice saying why.
.SH SESSION SHELL
Your login shell when hi styles it (bash, zsh, or fish), else the first of
fish > zsh > bash on the target. A target with no bash gets the first of
fish > zsh > dash > ash > sh, with hi's aliases (and in dash, ash, or sh a
colored prompt) but no header.
.SH TARGET RESOLUTION
.I target
is tried against each backend in order; the first match wins:
.TP
.B 1. ssh host
a literal
.B Host
entry in
.B ~/.ssh/config
(wildcards do not count).
.TP
.B 2. container
a running container, by name or ID, on the first of docker, podman, nerdctl,
and finch that has it; docker and podman also take a compose service name
with one running container.
.TP
.B 3. nomad allocation
a running allocation, by ID or ID prefix.
.TP
.B 4. kubernetes pod
a running pod in the current
.BR kubectl (1)
context and namespace, or
.I namespace:pod
or
.I context:namespace:pod
for one elsewhere.
.PP
.I alloc/task
and
.I pod/container
pick one task or container
.RB ( "nomad alloc exec \-task" ,
.BR "kubectl exec \-c" ).
Without it
.BR nomad (1)
refuses a multi\-task allocation and
.BR kubectl (1)
takes the pod's first container with a warning. Completion offers the pairs
and the prefixed pod names.
.PP
A name matching none of these still goes to
.BR ssh (1),
so unlisted hosts work.
.BR \-\-use " " \fIbackend\fR
skips every probe: the fix for a container that shadows an ssh host of the
same name, or the other way around.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
print the usage summary (synopsis, target resolution, hi's options) and exit.
Answered anywhere ahead of the target, never passed to
.BR ssh (1);
a word after it is refused.
.TP
.B \-V\fR, \fB\-\-version
print the version (the packaging stamp, or in a checkout its release tag,
with
.BI + N
for
.I N
commits past it), then the kind of tree (checkout, package, or tree) and its
path, and exit. The one ssh short option hi claims, since a bug report asks
for it first; a word after it is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-use \fIbackend\fR
force that arm and skip every probe:
.B ssh
(ssh's own error speaks if the host is not there), or one of
.BR docker ,
.BR podman ,
.BR nerdctl ,
.BR finch ,
.BR nomad ,
and
.BR kube .
Any other name, or two different ones, is an error.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
a bare shell on whatever
.I target
resolves to: no copy, no aliases, no header. The target needs only a shell
(and
.BR sshd (8)
for ssh) \- no
.BR tar (1),
no
.BR base64 (1),
no writable
.B /tmp
or
.BR $HOME .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-plain
hi's own session for this connect, past
.B _HI_PLAIN=1
in settings.sh or in the file of a tag the target carries. The last of
.B \-\-plain
and
.B \-\-no\-plain
wins.
.TP
.B \-\-mux
run the session in a local
.BR tmux (1),
.BR zellij (1),
or
.BR screen (1)
session, the first on your PATH, named
.BI hi\- target
(characters tmux rejects become dashes), so a dropped link leaves it running
and the next
.B hi \-\-mux
to that target reattaches. Client\-side only. Inside tmux hi switches to the
session; inside screen or zellij it opens a window or tab. With no terminal,
or no multiplexer, hi connects as usual.
.B _HI_MUX=1
makes this the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-mux
connect without the multiplexer, past
.B _HI_MUX=1
or an earlier
.BR \-\-mux ;
the last of the two flags wins.
.TP
.B \-\-keep
keep the session on an ssh target past a disconnect: it runs in a
.BR tmux (1),
.BR zellij (1),
or
.BR screen (1)
session there, the first of the three the target has, named
.BI hi\- target\fR,
and every later
.B hi
to that target reattaches instead of starting over. Detach with the
multiplexer's own key. Leaving the first pane's shell asks before it closes
the session; with nobody attached for
.B _HI_KEEP_TIMEOUT
(24h) it closes itself. Either way the session directory is removed.
The target needs bash and one of the three multiplexers, and you a terminal;
otherwise hi says so and connects as usual.
Panes opened in the session start hi's session shell as well, and close
with the first.
The directory of a kept session that died with its target is removed by the
next
.B hi
to it, which says the session is gone if this machine had seen it.
In a pane of a local multiplexer, a dropped link to a kept session is retried
for
.B _HI_KEEP_RETRY
(5m).
Typed with no target inside a session,
.B hi \-\-keep
keeps that session: a fresh shell in a multiplexer there, under the same
name; detaching returns to the shell it was typed in.
.B _HI_KEEP=1
makes this the default.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-keep
an ordinary session, past
.B _HI_KEEP=1
or an earlier
.BR \-\-keep ,
and beside any session the target is already keeping.
.TP
.B \-\-end
close the session
.I target
is keeping, without attaching to it. Exits 1 when there is none.
.PP
The local commands act on this machine instead of connecting. Each needs
.BR scripts/ ,
which a package ships and a session does not, so inside a session each says
so and stops.
.B \-\-update
also needs
.BR .git ,
which a package has not.
.BR \-n " is " \-\-dry\-run 's
short form wherever it appears.
.TP
.B \-\-preview \fR{\fBcolors\fR|\fBpackages\fR|\fBheader\fR}
print one of these and exit:
.B colors
\- each user and ssh host a pin, tag, or pattern colors, in that color, with
the rule it matched;
.B packages
\- one legend row per group, whether it runs, its colors, and an example,
then the marks and the check;
.B header
\- the connect header at your settings.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor \fR[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-problems\fR] [\fB\-\-use\fR \fIbackend\fR] [\fIssh\-options\fR] [\fItarget\fR]
print a read\-only pre\-flight report instead of connecting: the local tree;
the config overlay; each overlay member and the tool that reads it, with the
places it was found (the overlay, a home location, the tree's default) and
which one is used, or why none is; every rc and overlay shell file, parsed by the shell that
reads it; the install (each shell's rc lines and whether they name this tree,
the
.B hi
link, what
.B hi
on PATH runs, the file a login bash or a ZDOTDIR zsh really reads); every
backend, probed and timed; and, with a
.IR target ,
the backend it resolves to plus an ssh reachability check. Each section is a
table whose rows are marked by severity \- a check mark for ok,
.B !
for warn, a cross for bad
.RB ( + ,
.BR ! ,
and
.B x
outside a UTF\-8 locale).
.B \-\-problems
prints only the warn and bad rows, gathered into one findings table.
ssh options reach
that check's BatchMode probe as they would a connect, and are reported as
ignored for a non\-ssh target.
.B \-\-use
forces the backend as a connect would.
.BR \-\-plain ,
.BR \-\-no\-plain ,
.BR \-\-mux ,
.BR \-\-no\-mux ,
.BR \-\-keep ,
.BR \-\-no\-keep ,
and
.B \-\-end
are ignored, so a connect line pastes behind
.BR \-\-doctor .
.B \-\-json
prints the same rows as one JSON document on stdout, with the finding count
as
.BR findings .
.TP
.B \-\-install \fR[\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-link\fR {\fBnone\fR|\fBuser\fR|\fBsystem\fR}] [\fB\-\-shell\fR \fIlist\fR] [\fB\-\-print\-rc\fR] [\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
add or repair say-hi's lines in your shells' rc files (the login shell's, and
that of any other of bash, zsh, and fish with an rc file already; on macOS
also a
.B ~/.bash_profile
line so a login bash reads
.BR ~/.bashrc ),
and link
.BR hi .
Each line tests for the tree before sourcing it. A first install asks a
terminal whether hi styles this machine too, and nothing else:
.B \-\-configure
has the menu. An rc file that fails its syntax check stops the install:
with a question at a terminal, outright without one;
.BR \-y / \-\-yes
installs anyway.
.B \-\-link
places the link:
.B user
.RB ( ~/.local/bin/hi ,
the default),
.B system
.RB ( /usr/bin/hi ,
through sudo), or
.B none
(the wired shells alias
.B hi
regardless; the link is for scripts). No link is made when a
.B hi
on PATH already runs this tree, and a link owned by a package or anyone else
is left alone.
.B \-\-shell
.I list
names the shells instead:
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
and
.BR fish ,
a comma apart, or
.B all
for every one installed.
.B \-\-print\-rc
prints each rc file's lines and writes none, for an rc file a dotfile
manager owns: the block names the tree through
.BR $HOME ,
so one rc serves every machine,
.B \-\-doctor
reads it as wired, and a later install leaves it alone. An rc file hi
cannot write is named, with its lines, and the run ends at 1.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
answers the settings as under
.B \-\-configure
and asks nothing.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints every write (rc lines, settings.sh, the link) and makes none.
.B scripts/install.sh \-\-prefix
.I dir
is packaging mode: stage the tree under
.I dir
with
.B /usr/bin/hi
and
.BR /etc/profile.d/say\-hi.sh ,
touching no home directory.
.TP
.B \-\-uninstall \fR[\fB\-\-purge\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
remove those rc lines (and a macOS
.B ~/.bash_profile
the install created, if nothing else was written to it), settings.sh, and a
.B ~/.local/bin/hi
or
.B /usr/bin/hi
link that points at this tree.
.B \-\-purge
removes the whole overlay
.RB ( ~/.config/say-hi )
too. The tree stays. A one\-time
.B .hi\-orig
rc backup goes once the rc matches it again; one that differs stays, and the
lines that differ are printed.
.TP
.B \-\-configure \fR[\fB\-\-preset\fR \fIname\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
edit the settings alone, leaving the rc wiring and the link be: a numbered
menu under a live preview of the header and prompt, grouped by what a
setting changes: Header (its items to toggle and reorder, header presets,
width, hidden addresses, right under the header they change), Package
check (its groups), Prompt, Plugins, Aliases, This machine, and Advanced.
.B p
picks a preset
.RB ( everything ,
.BR balanced ,
.BR minimal ,
.BR lean ),
.B s
saves, and
.B q
quits without writing; nothing is written before either.
.B \-\-preset
.I name
applies one without the menu;
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the settings.sh it would write and writes nothing.
Before the menu, here and under
.BR \-\-install ,
a packages, colors, or settings.sh file in an older hi's shape is converted
to the current one, the original kept as
.IB file .old\fR;
.B \-\-dry\-run
names the conversion instead.
.TP
.B \-\-update \fR[\fItag\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
fetch the tags and check out the newest release, detached, or
.I tag
to move forward or back. On the
.B dev
branch with no
.IR tag ,
it fast-forwards dev to its upstream instead, and refuses a dev that has
diverged. A tree with uncommitted changes is refused.
.B \-\-dry\-run
fetches and names the tag, or counts the commits a pull would bring, without
moving. After the checkout or pull, the new tree's
converter rewrites any overlay file in an older shape, as under
.BR \-\-configure .
Needs a checkout: a package has no
.BR .git .
.TP
.B \-\-add\-package \fIgroup pkg\fR[,...] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
add rows to
.IR group 's
table of
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/packages ,
creating the table when there is none, one argument per row: "bat,batcat"
is bat, or batcat as a fallback, written bat = ["batcat"]; a leading \- marks
a package you don't want and lands the row in the group's .unwanted table, a
leading + one you need, in its .required table. A row whose first package is
already in the file
replaces that row, moving it to
.IR group .
That file replaces the tree's wholesale, so the first write copies the tree's
in first.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-package \fIpkg\fR... [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
remove the row whose first package is
.IR pkg ,
with or without its leading \- or +, from
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/packages ,
whichever group holds it; nothing else in the file changes. The first write
copies the tree's in, as under
.BR \-\-add\-package .
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-add\-tag \fIhost tag\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
write
.RI "# Tags: " tag
directly above
.IR host 's
.B Host
(or
.BR "Match host" )
line in
.BR ~/.ssh/config ,
or in the file an
.B Include
brings it from, so a
.B hosttag
or
.B usertag
row in the colors file colors it. A tag already there is replaced.
.I host
is matched as written in the config, so a pattern such as
.B *.prod
tags its whole block; a name only a wildcard block covers is refused, naming
that block.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-set\-color \fItype name color\fR [\fIrrggbb\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
pin
.I name
to
.I color
in the
.BI [ type ]
table of
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/colors ,
replacing a pin it already has and creating the table when there is none.
.I type
is
.BR hosttag ,
.BR usertag ,
.BR username ,
or
.BR hostname ;
.I name
holds no space,
.BR # ,
or bracket, and a
.B hostname
name holding * or ? is a pattern;
.I color
is one of the twenty\-four color names
.B hi \-\-set\-color \-\-help
lists;
.IR rrggbb ,
six hex digits, is the pin's own 24\-bit color on a truecolor terminal.
That file replaces the tree's wholesale, so the first write copies the tree's
in first.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-unset\-color \fItype name\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
remove
.IR name 's
pin from the
.BI [ type ]
table of
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/colors ,
so it colors by the next rule down. The first write copies the tree's in, as
under
.BR \-\-set\-color .
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-plugins
list every plugin, the configs of a tool's hi carries to a target and points
the tool at: its name, its group, each member, and the file that rides, or
why none does. A plugin is a table of the tree's
.B config/plugins
or of
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/plugins .
.TP
.B \-\-plugin\-off \fIname\fR... [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
switch plugins off for every target: a plugin that is off sends no file and
sets nothing there, so the tool keeps the target's own config.
.I name
is a plugin, a group
.RB ( editors ,
.BR mux ,
.BR prompt ,
.BR cli ,
.BR shell ,
or a table of your own plugins file),
or a member, as
.B hi \-\-plugins
lists them, or one file of a member that is a directory
.RB ( extensions/10\-kube ).
The list is
.B _HI_PLUGINS_OFF
in
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh .
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-plugin\-on \fIname\fR... [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
take
.I name
back out of that list.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-add\-plugin \fIgroup name file\fR... [\fIkey\fR=\fIvalue\fR...] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
write the
.BI [ group . name ]
table of
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/plugins ,
for the configs of a tool hi does not know, or your own plugin for one it
does:
.I group
is the word that switches it with its kind,
.I name
its own word and its name in a report, and each
.I file
the name a config rides under. The keys are
.BR tool= ,
the command that reads them (left out,
.IR name ;
.B \-
for none),
.BR wire= ,
how a target's tool finds a file,
.BR home= ,
where a file is here,
.BR dialect= ,
.BR init= ,
the tool's shell hook (a command printing a shell's code,
.B {shell}
for the shell's name, run on a target that has the tool; a plugin with one
needs no file),
.BR prompt=yes ,
when that init draws the prompt, and
.BR default=off ,
for a plugin kept home until
.B \-\-plugin\-on
names it (see
.BR FILES ).
A table hi could not read is refused, with the reason.
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-remove\-plugin \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
remove the plugin
.IR name ,
its table and its files' tables, from
.BR ~/.config/say-hi/plugins .
.B \-\-dry\-run
says what would be written and writes nothing.
.PP
Everything else is passed through to
.BR ssh (1)
unchanged
.RB ( \-p ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-J ,
.BR \-o ,
and the rest). ssh has no option starting with two dashes, so an unknown one
is hi's error. An option that opens on a word takes it joined too
.RB ( \-\-use=docker ,
.BR \-\-preview=colors ,
.BR \-\-update=v1.0.0 );
one that opens on a switch or takes nothing refuses it
.RB ( \-\-doctor=json ,
.BR \-\-plain=1 ).
The first non\-option word is the target and everything after it is the
remote command, so
.B hi host ls \-la
reads as it does for ssh; one of hi's own options there is refused by name.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B a connect
the session's or remote command's own status, as
.BR ssh (1)
would return it. 1 when hi refuses before connecting: an unknown or misplaced
option, a bad or conflicting
.BR \-\-use ,
hi's own options with no target, or a local command inside a session.
ssh options with no target return what
.BR ssh (1)
does.
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
0 with nothing to report, 1 on any finding; the count is in the report (and
.BR \-\-json 's
.BR findings ),
never in the status.
.TP
.B the other local commands
0 on success, 1 otherwise; an rc file failing its syntax check stops
.B \-\-install
at 1 before it writes anything.
.SH SESSION COMMANDS
.TP
.B hi_info
an alias, in a session and in the wired local shells, printing where the
running say\-hi lives: its home, root, and launcher. A diagnostic, not part
of the 1.x contract.
.SH COMPLETION
bash, zsh, and fish all complete from one lister,
.BR common/targets.sh .
zsh's completion function is
.B common/_hi
on
.BR $fpath ;
hi runs no
.BR compinit :
the one your rc runs, before hi's block or after it, picks it up.
.TP
.B hi <TAB>
every target: the
.B Host
entries in
.B ~/.ssh/config
plus every running container, allocation, and pod, each with its backend's
symbol (the
.B _HI_SYMBOL_*
settings; bash shows it only when bash 4 or later lists the matches). The
list is reused for five seconds, each backend gets two seconds to answer, and
one named in
.B _HI_BACKENDS_OFF
is not asked.
zsh colors it by backend (ssh hosts yellow, containers blue, nomad green,
kubernetes light purple) through the
.B hi\-targets
tag's
.B list\-colors
style, which one of your own replaces.
.TP
.B hi \-\-<TAB>
hi's options, with their one\-line help where the shell has a column for it
(fish and zsh), probing no backend. A session is offered none of the local
commands, and a package install no
.BR \-\-update .
.TP
.B hi \-\-install \-\-<TAB>
behind a local command, that command's own switches;
.B \-\-use
completes the backends (on a connect too),
.B \-\-link
and
.B \-\-preset
their values,
.B \-\-add\-package
the packages file's groups,
.B \-\-remove\-package
the first package of each of its rows,
.B \-\-set\-color
and
.B \-\-unset\-color
the four pin types,
.B \-\-plugin\-off
the plugins and their groups,
.B \-\-plugin\-on
the ones that are off,
.B \-\-remove\-plugin
the members of your plugins file, and
.B hi \-\-update <TAB>
the release tags a checkout knows of.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives outside the tree, in
.B ~/.config/say-hi/
(under
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
when set), and is carried to every target; the files are listed under
.BR FILES .
.B hi \-\-configure
writes
.BR settings.sh ;
you create the rest, the tree's
.B config/
copy of a file being the default until you copy it here. Every setting is an
environment variable, tabulated with its default in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md .
The tree itself can be root\-owned (a package install): hi never writes into
it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/share/say-hi
the tree, in a package install; a git checkout (in a directory named
.BR say-hi )
works the same.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.sh
settings, written by
.BR "hi \-\-configure" .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/settings.\fItag\fB.sh
settings for the hosts whose
.B # Tags:
line names
.IR tag ,
in settings.sh's shape: read after it on a connect, and sent to that host
joined to it. Written by hand.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/colors
your copy of
.BR config/colors ,
used in place of the tree's: a
.BI [ type ]
line
.RB ( hosttag ,
.BR usertag ,
.BR username ,
.BR hostname )
opens a table of
.IR name " = \(dq" color " [" rrggbb ]\(dq
rows, in TOML.
.B hi \-\-set\-color
copies the tree's here on its first write.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/packages
your package check, in TOML, used in place of the tree's
.BR config/packages .
.B hi \-\-add\-package
copies the tree's here on its first write.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/*.old
the original of a
.BR packages ,
.BR colors ,
or
.B settings.sh
in an older hi's shape, kept when
.BR \-\-install ,
.BR \-\-configure ,
or
.B \-\-update
converts it.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/vim/vimrc, nvim/init.lua, helix/config.toml, helix/languages.toml, kak/kakrc, kak/colors/, nano/nanorc, emacs/init.el
replacements for vim's, neovim's, helix's, kakoune's, nano's, and emacs's configs, used
whole. Without one, hi carries the config each editor already reads here
.RB ( ~/.vimrc ,
.BR ~/.config/nvim/init.lua ,
.BR ~/.config/helix/config.toml ,
.BR ~/.config/helix/languages.toml ,
.BR ~/.config/kak/kakrc
and its
.BR colors/ ,
.BR ~/.nanorc ,
.BR ~/.emacs ,
and their alternates), else none. A line that reads a file no target
has is dropped and named by
.BR \-\-doctor ;
a
.B # hi-allow
line above it sends it anyway; a
.B # hi-quiet
line above it still drops it, but leaves
.B \-\-doctor
no row about it. A pair,
.B # hi-allow-start
and
.B # hi-allow-end
or
.B # hi-quiet-start
and
.BR "# hi-quiet-end" ,
decides every line inside it the same way; a start with no end decides
nothing and is named by
.BR \-\-doctor .
tmux, screen, micro, zellij, and the shell and prompt files
below get the same pass.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/tmux/tmux.conf, screenrc, micro/, zellij/
the same for tmux (its alias becomes
.BR "tmux \-f" ),
screen
.RB ( "screen \-c" ),
micro's
.BR settings.json ,
.BR bindings.json ,
and
.B init.lua
(read through
.BR \-config\-dir ),
and zellij's
.BR config.kdl ,
.BR layouts/ ,
and
.B themes/
(read through
.BR \-\-config\-dir ).
Without them hi carries
.BR ~/.tmux.conf ,
.B ~/.screenrc
(or
.BR $SCREENRC ),
and micro's and zellij's own config directories.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/aliases.sh
your own aliases, sourced after hi's, so one of the same name replaces hi's.
.BR _HI_*_OPTS ,
.BR _HI_*_BIN ,
.BR _HI_DISABLE_* ,
.BR _HI_TOOL_ALIASES ,
and
.B _HI_SUDO_ALIAS
values set here arrive too late: they belong in
.BR settings.sh .
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/plugins
a plugin for each tool hi does not know, or your own for one it does, in
TOML: a
.BI [ group . name ]
table of
.BR files ,
.BR tool ,
.BR wire ,
.BR home ,
and
.B dialect
keys, a quoted value each, and a
.BI [ group . name .\(dq file \(dq]
table under it for a file whose wire, home, or dialect differs. Each of the
.B files
is carried from the first of the
.BR : \-separated
.B home
paths that exists (each starting at
.BR / ,
.BR ~/ ,
or a variable's name), under its own name, with the
.B tool
installed here;
.B env:
and
.B envdir:
name the variables a target sets to it,
.B flag:
and
.B flagdir:
the command and flag a target aliases with it, and
.B xdg:
the command a target aliases with
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
set to the overlay, for a file no variable or flag reaches. Nothing in a
table is evaluated.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/extensions/
extensions in the aliases.sh subset, sourced in name order after the aliases
and before the prompt; one a shell cannot parse is skipped with a warning. An
extension's
.B _HI_SEGMENT
command is drawn in the prompt.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/header/
header cells of your own, in bash: a file each, named for its word, defining
.BI _hi_cell_ word
for
.B _HI_HEADER_ORDER
to name.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/bashrc, zshrc, config.fish
your per\-shell preferences, sourced after hi's own so yours win. They ride
only from here: hi never carries a
.B ~/.bashrc
or the like it finds at home.
.TP
.B ~/.config/say-hi/oh-my-posh.json, oh-my-posh.yaml, oh-my-posh.toml
oh\-my\-posh's config, carried to every target as its
.B POSH_CONFIG
when oh\-my\-posh draws the prompt there. Without one, the file
.B POSH_CONFIG
or an rc's
.B oh\-my\-posh init \-\-config
names is carried instead.
.TP
.B ~/.config/starship.toml, ~/.config/bat/config, ~/.config/eza/theme.yml
the starship, bat, and eza configs in force here (or wherever
.BR STARSHIP_CONFIG ,
.BR BAT_CONFIG_PATH ,
or
.B EZA_CONFIG_DIR
points), carried to every target \- starship's only when starship is a
prompt program a target is handed. A
.BR starship.toml ,
.BR bat/config ,
or
.B theme.yml
in
.B ~/.config/say-hi
is carried instead.
.TP
.B ~/.inputrc
readline's config (or the file
.B INPUTRC
names), carried to every target as its
.BR INPUTRC ;
an
.B inputrc
in
.B ~/.config/say-hi
is carried instead. An
.B $include
of anything but
.B /etc/inputrc
is dropped.
.TP
.B $RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH, $FZF_DEFAULT_OPTS_FILE, ~/.config/lazygit/config.yml
the rg, fzf, and lazygit configs in force here (lazygit's wherever
.B LG_CONFIG_FILE
points), carried to every target and named there by the same variables. A
.BR ripgreprc ,
.BR fzfrc ,
or
.B lazygit/config.yml
in
.B ~/.config/say-hi
is carried instead.
.TP
.B ~/.p10k.zsh, ~/.zshrc, ~/.bashrc, ~/.config/fish/fish_variables
read for the prompt programs a target is handed: powerlevel10k's config, the
oh\-my\-zsh, oh\-my\-bash, and bash\-it theme files
.B ZSH_THEME ,
.BR OSH_THEME ,
and
.B BASH_IT_THEME
name, and tide's
.B tide_*
universal variables, carried as
.BR p10k.zsh ,
.BR oh-my-zsh.zsh-theme ,
.BR oh-my-bash.theme.sh ,
.BR bash-it.theme.bash ,
and
.BR tide.vars ,
each replaced by a copy of that name in
.BR ~/.config/say-hi .
Unset,
.B _HI_PROMPT_TOOL
hands the prompt to every such program installed here;
.B _HI_PROMPT_TOOL=hi
keeps hi's own, and an entry written
.IB shell : program
is tried first by that shell alone, so
.B "_HI_PROMPT_TOOL=\(dqbash:starship hi\(dq"
is starship in bash and hi's prompt in zsh and fish.
.TP
.B ~/.ssh/config
read for ssh targets and their
.B # Tags:
comments. The tagged
.B Host
and
.B Match host
lines, and nothing else of their blocks, are carried as
.B ssh_tags
so a
.B hi
run from inside a session colors the next hop by its tag; a copy of that
name in
.B ~/.config/say-hi
is carried instead.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every setting is an environment variable (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
A few that matter here:
.TP
.B _HI_HOME
the directory holding the
.B say-hi
tree: derived from hi's own path when unset, and set by
.B /etc/profile.d/say-hi.sh
in a package install.
.TP
.B _HI_TRUECOLOR
.B 1
or
.BR 0 :
whether your terminal renders 24\-bit color. Unset, it is read off
.B COLORTERM
and shipped to the session, since ssh never forwards that.
.TP
.B _HI_MUX
.B 1
makes every connect a
.B \-\-mux
one;
.B \-\-no\-mux
overrides it once. Client\-side only.
.TP
.B _HI_KEEP
.B 1
makes every ssh connect a
.B \-\-keep
one;
.B \-\-no\-keep
overrides it once.
.TP
.B _HI_PLUGINS_ON
the plugins or groups that are off by default (the shipped
.BR hooks :
zoxide, atuin, direnv, mise) and should ride and run anyway;
.B \-\-plugin\-on
writes it.
.TP
.B _HI_PLAIN
.B 1
makes every connect a
.B \-\-plain
one;
.B \-\-no\-plain
overrides it once. Its place is a tag's settings file.
.TP
.B _HI_UPDATE_SIGNED
.B 1
has
.B \-\-update
refuse a release tag unless its signature verifies against a key the checkout
already trusts; unset, an unsigned tag or an unknown key is said and allowed.
.TP
.B _HI_BACKENDS_OFF
the backends hi never asks, a space or a comma apart:
.BR docker ,
.BR podman ,
.BR nerdctl ,
.BR finch ,
.BR nomad ,
.BR kube ,
or
.B all
for ssh alone. One named here is left out of target resolution, completion,
and the header's counts;
.B \-\-use
still reaches it by name.
.TP
.B _HI_DISABLE_CONTROLMASTER
.B 1
keeps hi from passing ssh its
.BR ControlMaster ,
.BR ControlPath ,
and
.B ControlPersist
options, so your ssh config's own decide; the bootstrap and the session are
then two connections, each authenticating.
.TP
.B _HI_KEEP_TIMEOUT
how long a kept session waits with nobody attached before it closes: seconds,
or a number with
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
or
.BR d ;
.B 0
is never. Default
.BR 24h .
Read on the target, so set it in
.BR settings.sh .
.TP
.B _HI_KEEP_RETRY
how long a connect inside a local tmux, zellij, or screen keeps retrying a
target whose kept session it lost the link to: a duration as above;
.B 0
is never. Default
.BR 5m .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B hi prod-db
a session on the ssh host
.BR prod\-db ,
with your prompt, colors, and aliases.
.TP
.B hi -J bastion internal 'df -h'
run a command on
.B internal
through a jump host.
.TP
.B hi 8f41c2
a session in the running container or allocation with that ID.
.TP
.B hi \-\-use docker web
the container
.BR web ,
even with an ssh host of the same name; no probe runs.
.TP
.B hi \-\-plain rescue
a bare shell on
.BR rescue ,
nothing copied: for a box with no writable
.B /tmp
or no
.BR tar (1).
.TP
.B hi \-\-doctor \-\-json prod-db > report.json
the pre\-flight report as a document for a bug report; the exit status says
whether anything was found.
.TP
.B hi
no target: print the usage summary and exit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
Every setting is in
.IR docs/SETTINGS.md ;
the README and the rest of the docs are at
.IR https://github.com/ivylikethevine/say-hi .
